                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “You’re like a diagram,” she said, finally. “If they needed a picture
for the article, it’s almost rude how clear you are.”
   I laughed, wet, and then she laughed, and for a minute it was just
the two of us laughing at 4:50 in the morning at the shape of my own
head.
   “I gotta go back,” she said. “My nine minutes were twenty minutes
ago, and Maria’s going to kill me.” But she didn’t hang up. “JC. I can’t
diagnose you. I’m not allowed and it wouldn’t be right. A video can’t
do it either. Go see a real person. A doctor, not a Honda.”
   “I know.”
   “You’re going to say you will.”
   “I’ll book it before the sun’s up.”
   “Promise me.”
   “I’m promising you. Not my mother.”
   She laughed. “Okay.” A pause with a vending machine in it. “And
babe. You’re fine. Nobody ever gave you the paperwork.”
   She hung up. I held the phone against my chest for a while.



Somewhere between the radiator’s third knock and the 4’s next pass,
the room changed its light.
   Not the lamp. The ceiling went the yellow of a hallway bulb at night,
and the wall by the headboard, which has had a water stain on it since I
was little, was clean. It was 1972. I knew the year from the light, a yel‐
low that doesn’t exist anymore. There was a boy in my bed. Seven,
maybe, in pajamas with a hole in the knee, sitting up against the head‐
board, tapping the wood with two fingers in a rhythm that wasn’t any
rhythm I knew. Quiet. Steady. The tap of someone who has done it be‐
fore.
   In the doorway stood a woman in a housedress holding a piece of
folded paper from school, with a man behind her, close, reading it over
her shoulder. She read it twice, the way my father read the Calm Index
